  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
